{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017vKpsd9Hzjv9Bm7fDUQLPx","type":"tool_result","content":"README.md:129:   7.2.2 — the staged patched-module sets cover only 7.1.4/7.2.0. The\nREADME.md:132:   fetch-and-build (running-kernel gate: 7.2.2, both nodes, worker first)\nREADME.md:168:2. **wire-fallback** — the 5 GbE wire, terminal, loudly logged, and stamped\nREADME.md:169:   into every receipt as `fn_transport_rung` so a wire night can never be\nREADME.md:345:attended RDMA fetch-and-build on 7.2.2 (worker first) + A/B per\nbench/run-matrix.sh:440:# data.transport.fn_transport_rung is rail0-sockets — a wire-fallback rung\nbench/run-matrix.sh:559:        \"fn_transport_rung\": os.environ.get(\"TP_RUNG\", \"\"),\nbench/run-matrix.sh:569:             \"when transport.fn_transport_rung is rail0-sockets.\"),\nhost/fn-env.sh:131:    # rail night (a wire-fallback bench.json does NOT satisfy the rail-sockets\nhost/fn-env.sh:136:    export FN_TRANSPORT_RUNG=wire-fallback\nhost/fn-env.sh:146:# nothing is staged for the running 7.2.2 kernels. The pin stays\ntests/test_host_tooling.py:143:        self.assertIn(\"wire-fallback\", self.env)\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:306:      \"goal\": \"AMENDED 2026-08-30: docs/MORNING.md currently holds the operator's 2026-08-29 day-run stop-state record — FIRST move that file verbatim to docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md (git mv, keep every byte), THEN write the new docs/MORNING.md per spec claim 7.1 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r7). This lane renders BEFORE the milestone checkpoints run (it sits early in the serial graph), so the receipt review table has two row kinds: receipts already banked at render time (build, weights, possibly smoke) carry their observed status and the bound they were graded against; every receipt that lands AFTER render is PRE-DECLARED — one row each for preflight, proxy, tp2, residency, fidelity, context, bench, and usb4stream, naming the file path, the bound scripts/receipts-verify.py grades it against, and how to read it. Three pre-declared rows are mandatory and specific: (a) results/receipts/usb4stream.json — cp-usb4stream runs dead-last after cp-close and only when the pair serve is already down; name its three typed outcomes (ok / skipped:REASON / aborted:PHASE:ERRNO), say that data.outcome is where to read them, that 'skipped:serve-up-on-shared-cable' is the EXPECTED outcome after a healthy night, and that a skipped or aborted outcome is the typed blocker for that step per F.12; (b) results/receipts/failed/ — the quarantine directory: any step that fails its own grading writes its fail receipt THERE so one graded failure cannot redden every later gate run; every file present there in the morning is a typed blocker the operator reviews first; (c) results/receipts/failed/context.json — if present, the honest full-context decode ratio undershot the 0.9 bound (12 full-attention layers walking 256K KV; the community precedent shows about a 2x falloff by 50-73k) — a typed performance finding routed to the optimization menu, deliberately NOT a campaign failure. Also render: the open-items list (every UNKNOWN from the spec with its drain state); the promotion checklist (what the operator verifies before any fleet change, per ruling P13 — and state the causality plainly: the fleet's local-models sync retires any staged artifact that has no catalog row; it pruned the staged checkpoint from BOTH nodes on 08-29 around 20:07/20:14; applying handoff/catalog-row.patch is the act that makes staging durable, and it must precede any rebuild or any restart of the model-sync trigger service); the transport-rung caveat (if any receipt records fn_transport_rung wire-fallback, the night ran on the 5GbE wire: valid but degraded receipts, and bench.json then does NOT satisfy the rail-sockets Gate 0 for verbs bring-up — healing rail 0 is the morning's first transport act); the optimization menu; and a blocker template implementing ruling P16. The optimization menu carries, beyond the original items (tuned mixture-kernel config generation for this GPU, graph-capture matrix, jumbo-frame A-B, draft-length sweep, the four-bit expert lane pointers from IMPORTS.md): the speculative-head depth sweep n in 1 to 4 plus the index-share and padded-drafter knobs (UNKNOWN-5); the attended RDMA go/no-go with the honest cost (2-4 attended hours, two more reboots, worker deploy-path fix first, nothing staged for kernel 7.2.2 yet) and honest expectation (about +3.4 percent measured precedent over held TCP); the stream-primitive morning decision rule (attended port of the reference doorbell allreduce onto the in-tree stream device ONLY if the banked exchange p50 at 8-16 KiB is at or under about 40 microseconds AND the bench matrix shows decode is allreduce-dominated — noting the stream bench likely runs attended in the morning after teardown, since it skips while the pair serves); the full-context decode probe follow-up when deferred; a rejected-with-reason register so nothing is re-litigated (the community int4-WMMA expert lane: bound to a different runtime's artifact, single-device only, unreachable even in its own public release; the indexer rotation plus FP4 QAT art: fidelity art for a different model family's trained indexer graph — our engine's indexer is weight-free bf16 with no such trained graph, so transplanting it would corrupt top-k selection); the load-phase page-cache drop-behind remedy pointer for repo issue 2 (release file pages in chunks behind the loader); a per-shape kernel block-size tuning item for the 2560x640 and 640x2560 expert shapes (community precedent: a forced tile choice beat the auto-selector on exactly these shapes); and a note to audit the drafter hidden-state versus prefix-cache position sync before the speculative profile is declared production (a community v1.1 fix records the failure mode: cached target prefix reused while the drafter state timeline sits elsewhere breaks multi-turn — the operator files the repo issue). Also write docs/ANNOUNCE.md: a short community post draft describing the setup (two 128 GB unified-memory desktops, two fast rails, one control wire), what was built overnight, where the evidence lives, and where benchmarks land. Boundaries: touch only docs/MORNING.md, docs/ANNOUNCE.md, and docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:391:      \"goal\": \"RE-SCOPED 2026-08-30 (second flow): the package files under host/rdma ALREADY EXIST on main and pass this lane's original acceptance argv verbatim — do NOT rewrite them wholesale, and do not end with an empty diff (the empty-diff completion failure is a known harness hazard). Concrete deliverables now: (1) tests/test_rdma_package.py — the test the original goal promised: assert both pin commits 503c5ae1e72aa9ed91925dafa3d82ee2e992747f and 76ba39b630a70accb72f19388eefe48844b50eb8 appear in host/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh; assert no line of that script begins with insmod or modprobe (optionally sudo-prefixed) — the no-unattended-load invariant, spec F.1; assert host/rdma/attended-bringup.md carries the 'physically present' and 'worker first' language. (2) Fold the odinlink learnings (repo issue 6, evidence at the reference estate the issue names) into host/rdma/ab-protocol.md and host/rdma/attended-bringup.md as short dated appendix sections: terminal-failure framing for verbs init (on ANY init failure: no retry, restore the socket env byte-identical on both ranks, one restart, and the terminal fallback rung is ALWAYS the ethernet wire, never the second rail); the 11 microsecond one-way rail reference figure; the per-cable p2p ceiling asymmetry; and the measured hazard that a verbs transmit toward a closed peer receive ring stalls end-to-end credits and wedges the whole XDomain INCLUDING TCP on the same cable — recovery is reboot-only, which is why no verbs rung may ever appear in an unattended fallback ladder. (3) A dated 'state of tonight' preamble note in attended-bringup.md: no ibverbs device exists on either node right now, the staged module sets cover only kernels 7.1.4 and 7.2.0 while the fleet runs 7.2.2, so the attended morning fetch-and-build on BOTH nodes (running-kernel gate 7.2.2) precedes any A/B; the honest expectation from the one measured community precedent is about +3.4 percent over held TCP — adopt only on a fingerprint-clean counterbalanced majority win. The preamble must ALSO state the Gate 0 transport-rung caveat: the banked socket-transport benchmark that unlocks bring-up must have been measured over the Thunderbolt rail — a bench receipt whose transport record reads fn_transport_rung wire-fallback is a 5GbE artifact and does NOT satisfy Gate 0; on a wire-fallback night the attended morning first heals rail 0 and re-banks a rail-sockets bench before any verbs work. Keep every existing hard rule intact: nothing GPL vendored, nothing installs or loads automatically, one rail only, bring-up starts only after a committed socket-transport benchmark exists under results/. Boundaries: touch only host/rdma and tests/.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:394:        \"Tomorrow's attended bring-up doc tells the truth about kernel 7.2.2, the missing verbs devices, the wedge blast radius, and which transport rung a Gate 0 benchmark must carry\"\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:417:          \"description\": \"The pin test exists and passes; the odinlink fold, the 7.2.2 truth, and the wire-rung Gate 0 caveat landed in the docs.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:421:            \"python3 -m unittest tests.test_rdma_package -v && grep -qi 'odinlink' host/rdma/ab-protocol.md && grep -q '7.2.2' host/rdma/attended-bringup.md && grep -qi 'wire-fallback' host/rdma/attended-bringup.md\"\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:588:      \"goal\": \"Measure whether the ROCm 10.0.0 substrate is viable for this fork on this hardware, without touching the 7.14 lane that served the night. Context: a complete aligned gfx1151/cp312 wheel set exists at https://stable.repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-next/ (verified 2026-08-30; wheels dated 2026-08-26; same upstream versions and the SAME triton git hash 4cff872c as the current pins, so the swap is a pure version-literal substitution) — but no upstream anywhere has run this engine on ROCm 10 on gfx1151, and two failure modes are unmeasured: whether ROCm 10's hipcc compiles the fork's HIP sources, and whether its HSA runtime binds to the in-tree KFD on this kernel. This lane answers both as DATA. Build a second image flashnext:rocm10 from the UNMODIFIED container/Containerfile using its existing build ARGs (ROCM_WHL, TORCH_PIN, TORCHVISION_PIN, TRITON_PIN, ROCM_DEVEL_PIN, DEVICE_PINS at container/Containerfile:48-65 — no recipe edit, so the banked container-recipe acceptance stays true): podman build --tag flashnext:rocm10 --file container/Containerfile --build-arg ROCM_WHL=https://stable.repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-next/ --build-arg 'TORCH_PIN=torch==2.13.0+rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'TORCHVISION_PIN=torchvision==0.28.0+rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'TRITON_PIN=triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'ROCM_DEVEL_PIN=rocm-sdk-devel==10.0.0' --build-arg 'DEVICE_PINS=amd-torch-device-gfx1151==2.13.0+rocm10.0.0 amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151==0.28.0+rocm10.0.0 rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151==10.0.0' . (a prefetched wheel set may exist under ~/.cache/flashnext-wheels/rocm10/). Then, on the coordinator only, run a minimal GPU binding check in that image (--device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host): torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).gcnArchName, a small fp16 and a small fp8-block matmul, and importing the engine package. Record the ROCm/HSA and KFD versions the runtime reports plus the running kernel release (a green probe on a 7.2.0 coordinator is strong but not conclusive for the pair; a red probe attributable to KFD must be re-checked on 7.2.2 before ROCm 10 is written off — say so in the notes field). Do not load the checkpoint, do not start a server, do not touch the worker. WRITE THE RECEIPT TO results/rocm10-probe.json — NOT under results/receipts/: scripts/receipts-verify.py fails the campaign gate on any fail-status receipt in that directory, and this lane exists to record an outcome, green or red, never to fail the campaign. The receipt carries {step:'rocm10-probe', status, ts, data:{build_ok, compile_seconds, torch, triton, rocm_sdk_core, gcn_arch, cuda_available, matmul_fp16_ok, matmul_fp8_block_ok, engine_import_ok, kernel_release, notes}} with status 'pass' whenever an outcome was recorded. Never remove or retag flashnext:dev; never edit container/, results/receipts/, or the worklist. If the night is running late this lane is the first thing the operator drops. Boundaries: touch only results/rocm10-probe.json.\",\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:27:> 7.2.2 kernel move, and verbs failures wedge TCP on the same cable; RDMA is\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:82:kernel 7.1.4; the fleet has since moved to 7.2.2 and the staged out-of-tree\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:84:hard-gates on the *running* kernel being 7.2.2 — and the coordinator was still\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:185:2. **wire-fallback** — the 5GbE `enp191s0`, terminal, loud, receipted.\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:192:**What flips this decision:** tomorrow's attended bring-up on 7.2.2 (both\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:195:records `fn_transport_rung: rail0-sockets`, because a wire-fallback bench is a\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:532:7.2.0 pre-reboot, worker 7.2.2) — which is a TP=2-shaped failure that would\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:689:**8.4 No ibverbs device survives on 7.2.2** — see §2.1. The README's previous\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:829:7. **The RDMA userspace/kernel pin match on 7.2.2** — unanswerable tonight by\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:82:# 7.2.2: deliberate pin update 2026-08-30 — the twins ride nixpkgs-fresh's\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:83:# linuxPackages_7_2 (dotfiles#244), bumped to the 7.2.2 point release; the\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:85:# re-bake honest: it refuses to build until the box actually runs 7.2.2,\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:88:# on 7.2 headers) is superseded the moment 7.2.2 boots; earlier staged\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:90:TARGET_KVER=\"${TARGET_KVER:-7.2.2}\"\ndocs/ANNOUNCE.md:109:- Transport rung of record: `[FILL: rail0-sockets | wire-fallback]`\ndocs/MORNING.md:26:3. **Check the transport rung** — `jq -r '.data.transport.fn_transport_rung'\ndocs/MORNING.md:27:   results/receipts/bench.json`. If it reads `wire-fallback`, see\ndocs/MORNING.md:83:| `results/receipts/bench.json` | `bench/run-matrix.sh` (cp-bench) | `data.loads_per_arm >= 3`; `data.counterbalanced == true` — *unless* `data.spec_on_failed`, which then requires `data.arms == [\"spec_off\"]` | Claim 6.3 / ruling P12: three loads per arm, interleaved arms, medians, token fingerprints, depths 0 / 10240 / 102400, spec-on and spec-off arms. Rows land under `results/`. An interim receipt is banked right after the measurement sweep, so a runtime kill cannot erase the numbers. `data.transport.fn_transport_rung` is this receipt's Gate 0 provenance. If `spec_on_failed` is true the night is still honest — a labelled single-arm result and an attended `FN_SPEC_ARGS` iteration, not a lost night. |\ndocs/MORNING.md:153:jq -r '.data.transport.fn_transport_rung' results/receipts/bench.json\ndocs/MORNING.md:159:and `wire-fallback` (the 5 GbE control wire, terminal, loudly logged). Never\ndocs/MORNING.md:162:**If any receipt records `wire-fallback`, the night ran on the 5 GbE wire.**\ndocs/MORNING.md:186:| **UNKNOWN-3** — does the collective library cross the pair at TP=2 over socket transport? | claim 4.2 | Open at render. Read `tp2.json` — and read `fn_transport_rung` with it, since the answer differs per rung. |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"7b8c73b5-1fc8-4b02-8347-428f7e930e54","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:46:45.949Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"README.md:129:   7.2.2 — the staged patched-module sets cover only 7.1.4/7.2.0. The\nREADME.md:132:   fetch-and-build (running-kernel gate: 7.2.2, both nodes, worker first)\nREADME.md:168:2. **wire-fallback** — the 5 GbE wire, terminal, loudly logged, and stamped\nREADME.md:169:   into every receipt as `fn_transport_rung` so a wire night can never be\nREADME.md:345:attended RDMA fetch-and-build on 7.2.2 (worker first) + A/B per\nbench/run-matrix.sh:440:# data.transport.fn_transport_rung is rail0-sockets — a wire-fallback rung\nbench/run-matrix.sh:559:        \"fn_transport_rung\": os.environ.get(\"TP_RUNG\", \"\"),\nbench/run-matrix.sh:569:             \"when transport.fn_transport_rung is rail0-sockets.\"),\nhost/fn-env.sh:131:    # rail night (a wire-fallback bench.json does NOT satisfy the rail-sockets\nhost/fn-env.sh:136:    export FN_TRANSPORT_RUNG=wire-fallback\nhost/fn-env.sh:146:# nothing is staged for the running 7.2.2 kernels. The pin stays\ntests/test_host_tooling.py:143:        self.assertIn(\"wire-fallback\", self.env)\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:306:      \"goal\": \"AMENDED 2026-08-30: docs/MORNING.md currently holds the operator's 2026-08-29 day-run stop-state record — FIRST move that file verbatim to docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md (git mv, keep every byte), THEN write the new docs/MORNING.md per spec claim 7.1 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r7). This lane renders BEFORE the milestone checkpoints run (it sits early in the serial graph), so the receipt review table has two row kinds: receipts already banked at render time (build, weights, possibly smoke) carry their observed status and the bound they were graded against; every receipt that lands AFTER render is PRE-DECLARED — one row each for preflight, proxy, tp2, residency, fidelity, context, bench, and usb4stream, naming the file path, the bound scripts/receipts-verify.py grades it against, and how to read it. Three pre-declared rows are mandatory and specific: (a) results/receipts/usb4stream.json — cp-usb4stream runs dead-last after cp-close and only when the pair serve is already down; name its three typed outcomes (ok / skipped:REASON / aborted:PHASE:ERRNO), say that data.outcome is where to read them, that 'skipped:serve-up-on-shared-cable' is the EXPECTED outcome after a healthy night, and that a skipped or aborted outcome is the typed blocker for that step per F.12; (b) results/receipts/failed/ — the quarantine directory: any step that fails its own grading writes its fail receipt THERE so one graded failure cannot redden every later gate run; every file present there in the morning is a typed blocker the operator reviews first; (c) results/receipts/failed/context.json — if present, the honest full-context decode ratio undershot the 0.9 bound (12 full-attention layers walking 256K KV; the community precedent shows about a 2x falloff by 50-73k) — a typed performance finding routed to the optimization menu, deliberately NOT a campaign failure. Also render: the open-items list (every UNKNOWN from the spec with its drain state); the promotion checklist (what the operator verifies before any fleet change, per ruling P13 — and state the causality plainly: the fleet's local-models sync retires any staged artifact that has no catalog row; it pruned the staged checkpoint from BOTH nodes on 08-29 around 20:07/20:14; applying handoff/catalog-row.patch is the act that makes staging durable, and it must precede any rebuild or any restart of the model-sync trigger service); the transport-rung caveat (if any receipt records fn_transport_rung wire-fallback, the night ran on the 5GbE wire: valid but degraded receipts, and bench.json then does NOT satisfy the rail-sockets Gate 0 for verbs bring-up — healing rail 0 is the morning's first transport act); the optimization menu; and a blocker template implementing ruling P16. The optimization menu carries, beyond the original items (tuned mixture-kernel config generation for this GPU, graph-capture matrix, jumbo-frame A-B, draft-length sweep, the four-bit expert lane pointers from IMPORTS.md): the speculative-head depth sweep n in 1 to 4 plus the index-share and padded-drafter knobs (UNKNOWN-5); the attended RDMA go/no-go with the honest cost (2-4 attended hours, two more reboots, worker deploy-path fix first, nothing staged for kernel 7.2.2 yet) and honest expectation (about +3.4 percent measured precedent over held TCP); the stream-primitive morning decision rule (attended port of the reference doorbell allreduce onto the in-tree stream device ONLY if the banked exchange p50 at 8-16 KiB is at or under about 40 microseconds AND the bench matrix shows decode is allreduce-dominated — noting the stream bench likely runs attended in the morning after teardown, since it skips while the pair serves); the full-context decode probe follow-up when deferred; a rejected-with-reason register so nothing is re-litigated (the community int4-WMMA expert lane: bound to a different runtime's artifact, single-device only, unreachable even in its own public release; the indexer rotation plus FP4 QAT art: fidelity art for a different model family's trained indexer graph — our engine's indexer is weight-free bf16 with no such trained graph, so transplanting it would corrupt top-k selection); the load-phase page-cache drop-behind remedy pointer for repo issue 2 (release file pages in chunks behind the loader); a per-shape kernel block-size tuning item for the 2560x640 and 640x2560 expert shapes (community precedent: a forced tile choice beat the auto-selector on exactly these shapes); and a note to audit the drafter hidden-state versus prefix-cache position sync before the speculative profile is declared production (a community v1.1 fix records the failure mode: cached target prefix reused while the drafter state timeline sits elsewhere breaks multi-turn — the operator files the repo issue). Also write docs/ANNOUNCE.md: a short community post draft describing the setup (two 128 GB unified-memory desktops, two fast rails, one control wire), what was built overnight, where the evidence lives, and where benchmarks land. Boundaries: touch only docs/MORNING.md, docs/ANNOUNCE.md, and docs/DAYRUN-STOP-STATE-2026-08-29.md.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:391:      \"goal\": \"RE-SCOPED 2026-08-30 (second flow): the package files under host/rdma ALREADY EXIST on main and pass this lane's original acceptance argv verbatim — do NOT rewrite them wholesale, and do not end with an empty diff (the empty-diff completion failure is a known harness hazard). Concrete deliverables now: (1) tests/test_rdma_package.py — the test the original goal promised: assert both pin commits 503c5ae1e72aa9ed91925dafa3d82ee2e992747f and 76ba39b630a70accb72f19388eefe48844b50eb8 appear in host/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh; assert no line of that script begins with insmod or modprobe (optionally sudo-prefixed) — the no-unattended-load invariant, spec F.1; assert host/rdma/attended-bringup.md carries the 'physically present' and 'worker first' language. (2) Fold the odinlink learnings (repo issue 6, evidence at the reference estate the issue names) into host/rdma/ab-protocol.md and host/rdma/attended-bringup.md as short dated appendix sections: terminal-failure framing for verbs init (on ANY init failure: no retry, restore the socket env byte-identical on both ranks, one restart, and the terminal fallback rung is ALWAYS the ethernet wire, never the second rail); the 11 microsecond one-way rail reference figure; the per-cable p2p ceiling asymmetry; and the measured hazard that a verbs transmit toward a closed peer receive ring stalls end-to-end credits and wedges the whole XDomain INCLUDING TCP on the same cable — recovery is reboot-only, which is why no verbs rung may ever appear in an unattended fallback ladder. (3) A dated 'state of tonight' preamble note in attended-bringup.md: no ibverbs device exists on either node right now, the staged module sets cover only kernels 7.1.4 and 7.2.0 while the fleet runs 7.2.2, so the attended morning fetch-and-build on BOTH nodes (running-kernel gate 7.2.2) precedes any A/B; the honest expectation from the one measured community precedent is about +3.4 percent over held TCP — adopt only on a fingerprint-clean counterbalanced majority win. The preamble must ALSO state the Gate 0 transport-rung caveat: the banked socket-transport benchmark that unlocks bring-up must have been measured over the Thunderbolt rail — a bench receipt whose transport record reads fn_transport_rung wire-fallback is a 5GbE artifact and does NOT satisfy Gate 0; on a wire-fallback night the attended morning first heals rail 0 and re-banks a rail-sockets bench before any verbs work. Keep every existing hard rule intact: nothing GPL vendored, nothing installs or loads automatically, one rail only, bring-up starts only after a committed socket-transport benchmark exists under results/. Boundaries: touch only host/rdma and tests/.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:394:        \"Tomorrow's attended bring-up doc tells the truth about kernel 7.2.2, the missing verbs devices, the wedge blast radius, and which transport rung a Gate 0 benchmark must carry\"\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:417:          \"description\": \"The pin test exists and passes; the odinlink fold, the 7.2.2 truth, and the wire-rung Gate 0 caveat landed in the docs.\",\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:421:            \"python3 -m unittest tests.test_rdma_package -v && grep -qi 'odinlink' host/rdma/ab-protocol.md && grep -q '7.2.2' host/rdma/attended-bringup.md && grep -qi 'wire-fallback' host/rdma/attended-bringup.md\"\nsilent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json:588:      \"goal\": \"Measure whether the ROCm 10.0.0 substrate is viable for this fork on this hardware, without touching the 7.14 lane that served the night. Context: a complete aligned gfx1151/cp312 wheel set exists at https://stable.repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-next/ (verified 2026-08-30; wheels dated 2026-08-26; same upstream versions and the SAME triton git hash 4cff872c as the current pins, so the swap is a pure version-literal substitution) — but no upstream anywhere has run this engine on ROCm 10 on gfx1151, and two failure modes are unmeasured: whether ROCm 10's hipcc compiles the fork's HIP sources, and whether its HSA runtime binds to the in-tree KFD on this kernel. This lane answers both as DATA. Build a second image flashnext:rocm10 from the UNMODIFIED container/Containerfile using its existing build ARGs (ROCM_WHL, TORCH_PIN, TORCHVISION_PIN, TRITON_PIN, ROCM_DEVEL_PIN, DEVICE_PINS at container/Containerfile:48-65 — no recipe edit, so the banked container-recipe acceptance stays true): podman build --tag flashnext:rocm10 --file container/Containerfile --build-arg ROCM_WHL=https://stable.repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-next/ --build-arg 'TORCH_PIN=torch==2.13.0+rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'TORCHVISION_PIN=torchvision==0.28.0+rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'TRITON_PIN=triton==3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm10.0.0' --build-arg 'ROCM_DEVEL_PIN=rocm-sdk-devel==10.0.0' --build-arg 'DEVICE_PINS=amd-torch-device-gfx1151==2.13.0+rocm10.0.0 amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151==0.28.0+rocm10.0.0 rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151==10.0.0' . (a prefetched wheel set may exist under ~/.cache/flashnext-wheels/rocm10/). Then, on the coordinator only, run a minimal GPU binding check in that image (--device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host): torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).gcnArchName, a small fp16 and a small fp8-block matmul, and importing the engine package. Record the ROCm/HSA and KFD versions the runtime reports plus the running kernel release (a green probe on a 7.2.0 coordinator is strong but not conclusive for the pair; a red probe attributable to KFD must be re-checked on 7.2.2 before ROCm 10 is written off — say so in the notes field). Do not load the checkpoint, do not start a server, do not touch the worker. WRITE THE RECEIPT TO results/rocm10-probe.json — NOT under results/receipts/: scripts/receipts-verify.py fails the campaign gate on any fail-status receipt in that directory, and this lane exists to record an outcome, green or red, never to fail the campaign. The receipt carries {step:'rocm10-probe', status, ts, data:{build_ok, compile_seconds, torch, triton, rocm_sdk_core, gcn_arch, cuda_available, matmul_fp16_ok, matmul_fp8_block_ok, engine_import_ok, kernel_release, notes}} with status 'pass' whenever an outcome was recorded. Never remove or retag flashnext:dev; never edit container/, results/receipts/, or the worklist. If the night is running late this lane is the first thing the operator drops. Boundaries: touch only results/rocm10-probe.json.\",\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:27:> 7.2.2 kernel move, and verbs failures wedge TCP on the same cable; RDMA is\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:82:kernel 7.1.4; the fleet has since moved to 7.2.2 and the staged out-of-tree\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:84:hard-gates on the *running* kernel being 7.2.2 — and the coordinator was still\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:185:2. **wire-fallback** — the 5GbE `enp191s0`, terminal, loud, receipted.\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:192:**What flips this decision:** tomorrow's attended bring-up on 7.2.2 (both\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:195:records `fn_transport_rung: rail0-sockets`, because a wire-fallback bench is a\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:532:7.2.0 pre-reboot, worker 7.2.2) — which is a TP=2-shaped failure that would\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:689:**8.4 No ibverbs device survives on 7.2.2** — see §2.1. The README's previous\ndocs/DECISIONS-2026-08-30.md:829:7. **The RDMA userspace/kernel pin match on 7.2.2** — unanswerable tonight by\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:82:# 7.2.2: deliberate pin update 2026-08-30 — the twins ride nixpkgs-fresh's\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:83:# linuxPackages_7_2 (dotfiles#244), bumped to the 7.2.2 point release; the\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:85:# re-bake honest: it refuses to build until the box actually runs 7.2.2,\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:88:# on 7.2 headers) is superseded the moment 7.2.2 boots; earlier staged\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh:90:TARGET_KVER=\"${TARGET_KVER:-7.2.2}\"\ndocs/ANNOUNCE.md:109:- Transport rung of record: `[FILL: rail0-sockets | wire-fallback]`\ndocs/MORNING.md:26:3. **Check the transport rung** — `jq -r '.data.transport.fn_transport_rung'\ndocs/MORNING.md:27:   results/receipts/bench.json`. If it reads `wire-fallback`, see\ndocs/MORNING.md:83:| `results/receipts/bench.json` | `bench/run-matrix.sh` (cp-bench) | `data.loads_per_arm >= 3`; `data.counterbalanced == true` — *unless* `data.spec_on_failed`, which then requires `data.arms == [\"spec_off\"]` | Claim 6.3 / ruling P12: three loads per arm, interleaved arms, medians, token fingerprints, depths 0 / 10240 / 102400, spec-on and spec-off arms. Rows land under `results/`. An interim receipt is banked right after the measurement sweep, so a runtime kill cannot erase the numbers. `data.transport.fn_transport_rung` is this receipt's Gate 0 provenance. If `spec_on_failed` is true the night is still honest — a labelled single-arm result and an attended `FN_SPEC_ARGS` iteration, not a lost night. |\ndocs/MORNING.md:153:jq -r '.data.transport.fn_transport_rung' results/receipts/bench.json\ndocs/MORNING.md:159:and `wire-fallback` (the 5 GbE control wire, terminal, loudly logged). Never\ndocs/MORNING.md:162:**If any receipt records `wire-fallback`, the night ran on the 5 GbE wire.**\ndocs/MORNING.md:186:| **UNKNOWN-3** — does the collective library cross the pair at TP=2 over socket transport? | claim 4.2 | Open at render. Read `tp2.json` — and read `fn_transport_rung` with it, since the answer differs per rung. |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
