ork! What is the typical latency you get from Odinlink there?\n/home/tom/trans.md:936:● Fetch(https://github.com/wkljohn/OdinLink-Five)\n/home/tom/trans.md:950:● Cloned and read both repos plus OdinLink-Five. This changes one thing I told you earlier, and kills the TB5 idea for a reason that has nothing to do with Thunderbolt.\n/home/tom/trans.md:952:  OdinLink is the actual news\n/home/tom/trans.md:954:  I said USB4 gets you llama.cpp RPC pipeline-parallel but not tensor-parallel. That's now wrong. OdinLink-Five is an RDMA verbs provider (ibv_*, dma-buf zero-copy, RCCL plugin) that runs over your existing native\n/home/tom/trans.md:960:  │                           │         OdinLink / USB4         │ Mellanox RoCE v2 │\n=== 11 us / one-way ===","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"61342d50-bbc3-47ad-b12e-425f8a3ce5f7","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:10.901Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/home/tom/additional-research.md:147:| `ds4-strix-halo-tp-odinlink` | 0 | **0** | 0 | 0 | 20 |\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:158:Repo inventory note: there is **no standalone `odinlink` clone and no\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:160:`ds4-strix-halo-tp-odinlink`, which is the OdinLink *consumer*.\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:330:The direct A/B, same rig and settings: **TCP 8.29 decode vs OdinLink RDMA 8.57 — +3.4%**.\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:388:OdinLink is documented-broken with two cables (\"both peer services sit at route=2… use 1\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:414:7. **\"OdinLink is a candidate.\"** Documented-broken with two cables, which is the current\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:530:per-message completions. A third distinct design point alongside OdinLink and\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:546:| wkljohn, same rig/settings | TCP vs OdinLink RDMA decode | 8.29 → 8.57 t/s (**+3.4%**) |\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:556:### A4. ds4-vllm PR #4 — \"stock-kernel OdinLink fabric + decode/MTP perf work\"\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:562:**It deletes the patched-kernel requirement.** The author found OdinLink needs only two\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:571:single hardest thing to express cleanly in Nix. It is now moot for the OdinLink path.\n/home/tom/additional-research.md:582:| **`odl_mq`** — MessageQueue control plane moved from TCP to OdinLink streams | note this is the *opposite* direction from the 5GbE control-plane plan in §5; both remove IP from the TB rails, by different routes |\n/home/tom/trans.md:276:  https://github.com/wkljohn/ds4-strix-halo-tp-odinlink\n/home/tom/trans.md:287:  RDMA over USB4/TB5 or Mellanox RoCE v2. Q4_K setup without DSpark reaches up to 275 tok/s prefill and 21 tok/s decode. https://github.com/wkljohn/ds4-strix-halo-tp-odinlink\n/home/tom/trans.md:904:  Nice work! What is the typical latency you get from Odinlink there?\n/home/tom/trans.md:936:● Fetch(https://github.com/wkljohn/OdinLink-Five)\n/home/tom/trans.md:950:● Cloned and read both repos plus OdinLink-Five. This changes one thing I told you earlier, and kills the TB5 idea for a reason that has nothing to do with Thunderbolt.\n/home/tom/trans.md:952:  OdinLink is the actual news\n/home/tom/trans.md:954:  I said USB4 gets you llama.cpp RPC pipeline-parallel but not tensor-parallel. That's now wrong. OdinLink-Five is an RDMA verbs provider (ibv_*, dma-buf zero-copy, RCCL plugin) that runs over your existing native\n/home/tom/trans.md:960:  │                           │         OdinLink / USB4         │ Mellanox RoCE v2 │\n=== 11 us / one-way ===","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeYFKeZcHx5BLcDQ4BZR6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GKY1QH5onbho22RFEtYbyv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -rn -i \"11 *us\\|11 *µs\\|one-way\\|one way\\|24 *Gb\\|ceiling\" /home/tom/trans.md | sed -n 1,60p","description":"Search trans.md for latency figures"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1373,"cache_read_input_tokens":74016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1373},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"ba92e244-12d3-4f86-b967-14711733b00b","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:13.534Z","request_id":"req_011CeYFKdLSUSaTcbE5zWpFf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GKY1QH5onbho22RFEtYbyv","type":"tool_result","content":"/home/tom/trans.md:393:  someone did hit 410 prefill /s, im not sure what their launch parametres were though, im running a 64GB box so i cant measure the ceiling prefill,\n/home/tom/trans.md:532:  11 μs according to this (One way), compared to 1.4 µs for CX7 on the spark.\n/home/tom/trans.md:1080:  MiB/s, which is right at the x4 Gen3 ceiling. It also leaves the mainboard's PCIe slot untouched.\n/home/tom/trans.md:1616:  │   Model shape    │      Bytes read/token       │ Strix Halo ceiling @215 GB/s │\n/home/tom/trans.md:1631:  The validation is in q38rocm's own numbers: FP4 is 13.55 GB, ceiling 15.9 t/s, measured 14.02 t/s unassisted — 88% of theoretical. The implementation is efficient; the bandwidth wall is real.\n/home/tom/trans.md:1665:  - ~7 µs one-way at 64 B; ~48 Gb/s per direction, ~95 Gb/s bidirectional at 4-HCA aggregate\n/home/tom/trans.md:1733:  But the software ceiling binds before the topology does. The DS4 fork states it outright: \"This fork is for dual-Strix-Halo TP=2 over OdinLink or RoCE v2.\" ds4-vllm is TP=2, box1 Ray head / box2 worker. And TP\n/home/tom/trans.md:1798:  An eGPU does fix it. R9700, 32 GB at 644.6 GB/s, ~$1,299: 26.25 GB ÷ 644.6 = 24.5 t/s ceiling, call it ~20 real, and MTP on top. The catch is context — 32 − 26.25 leaves only ~5.75 GB for KV, so you need\n/home/tom/trans.md:1956:  Hard Disk Size: 1024 GB\n/home/tom/trans.md:1957:  1024 GB\n/home/tom/trans.md:2154:       6 MoE ceilings each configuration actually reaches.\n/home/tom/trans.md:2219:  │ GPU / eGPU         │ Opus  │ Dense ceilings; dual 5090 vs single PRO 6000 quantified on x4                             │\n/home/tom/trans.md:2280:  │ GTT ceiling          │ 128 GiB (ttm.pages_limit=33554432), VRAM carve-out at the 512 MiB minimum. You're already above the 120 GB AMD documents.                                                              │\n/home/tom/trans.md:2674:  │    Interconnect    │       Ports/node       │                 Latency                 │ Clean node ceiling │   Costs    │\n/home/tom/trans.md:2700:  Measured: 32 t/s on Strix Halo alone → 51.1 t/s with the R9700 added, on a 284B model. And on dense, 61.3 t/s on Qwen3.8-27B with DFlash2 on the R9700 — against the ~8 t/s ceiling your Strix Halo has for a 27B at\n/home/tom/trans.md:2706:  You're right that it's marketing-heavy — top-k 4 instead of the model's default 6 is a quality trade they bury, sparse prefill isn't byte-exact, 55 t/s is a 53-token ceiling test, and 51.1 vs the 86 on their\n/home/tom/trans.md:2717:  if we go super exotic route, explain if this will still mean that each individual strix halo remains \"its own computer\" in a somewhat standalone way. this is a few paragraphs max\n/home/tom/trans.md:2889:  One pre-existing line is worth keeping, because it corroborates the throughput ceiling:\n/home/tom/trans.md:2893:  The kernel itself flags thunderbolt_net's receive offload as suspect. That is a driver-quality ceiling, not a link ceiling — and it is precisely what the RDMA stacks bypass.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3097:  Also worth noting from that same thread, a Framework Desktop owner: \"I tried TB networking on Framework Desktop, but it seems to be limited to 10 GB/s\" — the per-rail ceiling, independently hit by someone else on\n/home/tom/trans.md:3127:  cached bf16 dequant of the fp8 trunk) and what ROCmFPX proves works on this ISA at 88% of bandwidth ceiling. Whether vLLM's stock ROCm FP8 path does the smart thing on gfx1151 is unproven — AMD's reference\n/home/tom/trans.md:3161:  The thing worth internalising: in the last six months this hardware's ceiling moved 34 → 275 t/s prefill on unchanged silicon, and today your own link went 577 → 63 µs from a sysfs write. The gap between what\n/home/tom/trans.md:3276:  Dense at Q8: it fails your requirement, twice. 27B at Q8_0 is 28.7 GB against 32 GB of VRAM — about 3 GB left, which is roughly a 64K context ceiling at q4_0 KV, and ~17 t/s. Two R9700s cannot rescue this by\n/home/tom/trans.md:3304:  Nuance worth having: LLM decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, and a 300 W card at batch 1 may only pull 150–200 W anyway, so a 120 W ceiling may cost less than it sounds. Prefill is compute-bound and will take the\n/home/tom/trans.md:3438:  ceiling unpacking in VGPRs, and ds4-vllm already caches a BF16 dequant of an FP8 trunk. It's a software gap on a trodden path, not a wall.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3519:  ceiling unpacking in VGPRs, and ds4-vllm already caches a BF16 dequant of an FP8 trunk. It's a software gap on a trodden path, not a wall.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3530:  Register-time dequant. Weights stay FP8 in memory at 1 byte/param, unpacked to BF16 in VGPRs at the point of use. Footprint stays 172.78 GiB. This is what ROCmFPX does at 88% of bandwidth ceiling.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3625:  The card matters more than the switch. Your taps are PCIe 4.0 x4 (~63 Gbps ceiling), so you want a Gen4-capable card or the card's own link becomes the bottleneck. A ConnectX-3 is PCIe 3.0 x8 — in a Gen4 x4 slot\n/home/tom/trans.md:3631:  │ InfiniBand — SX6036    │ FDR 56 Gb/s, 36 ports │ ~€100–150          │ ~1.1 µs measured on Strix Halo │ Needs opensm; NCCL/RCCL speak IB natively. FDR 56 Gb ≈ your x4 ceiling — near-perfect match │\n/home/tom/trans.md:3639:  ceiling so nothing is wasted. The cost is running opensm and living with older silicon.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3659:  Decode ceiling: 18B active at FP8 ≈ 18 GB/token, quartered to 4.5 GB/node → ~21 ms → ~48 t/s before collectives. With an MTP layer and num_speculative_tokens: 5 on top.\n/home/tom/trans.md:4111:  Half the active parameters means roughly half the streamed bytes per token, which is roughly double the decode ceiling on a bandwidth-bound machine. That's an architecture advantage, not something waiting on\n/home/tom/trans.md:4139:  → 21.1 t/s ceiling; experts-sharded TP=2 = 8.91 GiB/token → 25.1 t/s ceiling. Halving the experts buys 16%. That is why Ran sees decode \"capped\" while prefill doubles.\n/home/tom/trans.md:4253:  Prefill will benefit. At a 2048-token chunk those same collectives carry 16 MB each — roughly 1.4 GB per chunk — and the per-rail ~10 Gb/s ceiling is genuinely binding. This is exactly the pain corruptedstack\n/home/tom/trans.md:4906:  I built the \"op assembly dominates wire time\" argument on those numbers. The structure survives — nhi_throttle.c:5-9 independently names 128 µs moderation against \"the measured ~65 µs mean one-way latency floor","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"cfbd0ef6-8d52-45fd-86b4-008735f22c66","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:15.631Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/home/tom/trans.md:393:  someone did hit 410 prefill /s, im not sure what their launch parametres were though, im running a 64GB box so i cant measure the ceiling prefill,\n/home/tom/trans.md:532:  11 μs according to this (One way), compared to 1.4 µs for CX7 on the spark.\n/home/tom/trans.md:1080:  MiB/s, which is right at the x4 Gen3 ceiling. It also leaves the mainboard's PCIe slot untouched.\n/home/tom/trans.md:1616:  │   Model shape    │      Bytes read/token       │ Strix Halo ceiling @215 GB/s │\n/home/tom/trans.md:1631:  The validation is in q38rocm's own numbers: FP4 is 13.55 GB, ceiling 15.9 t/s, measured 14.02 t/s unassisted — 88% of theoretical. The implementation is efficient; the bandwidth wall is real.\n/home/tom/trans.md:1665:  - ~7 µs one-way at 64 B; ~48 Gb/s per direction, ~95 Gb/s bidirectional at 4-HCA aggregate\n/home/tom/trans.md:1733:  But the software ceiling binds before the topology does. The DS4 fork states it outright: \"This fork is for dual-Strix-Halo TP=2 over OdinLink or RoCE v2.\" ds4-vllm is TP=2, box1 Ray head / box2 worker. And TP\n/home/tom/trans.md:1798:  An eGPU does fix it. R9700, 32 GB at 644.6 GB/s, ~$1,299: 26.25 GB ÷ 644.6 = 24.5 t/s ceiling, call it ~20 real, and MTP on top. The catch is context — 32 − 26.25 leaves only ~5.75 GB for KV, so you need\n/home/tom/trans.md:1956:  Hard Disk Size: 1024 GB\n/home/tom/trans.md:1957:  1024 GB\n/home/tom/trans.md:2154:       6 MoE ceilings each configuration actually reaches.\n/home/tom/trans.md:2219:  │ GPU / eGPU         │ Opus  │ Dense ceilings; dual 5090 vs single PRO 6000 quantified on x4                             │\n/home/tom/trans.md:2280:  │ GTT ceiling          │ 128 GiB (ttm.pages_limit=33554432), VRAM carve-out at the 512 MiB minimum. You're already above the 120 GB AMD documents.                                                              │\n/home/tom/trans.md:2674:  │    Interconnect    │       Ports/node       │                 Latency                 │ Clean node ceiling │   Costs    │\n/home/tom/trans.md:2700:  Measured: 32 t/s on Strix Halo alone → 51.1 t/s with the R9700 added, on a 284B model. And on dense, 61.3 t/s on Qwen3.8-27B with DFlash2 on the R9700 — against the ~8 t/s ceiling your Strix Halo has for a 27B at\n/home/tom/trans.md:2706:  You're right that it's marketing-heavy — top-k 4 instead of the model's default 6 is a quality trade they bury, sparse prefill isn't byte-exact, 55 t/s is a 53-token ceiling test, and 51.1 vs the 86 on their\n/home/tom/trans.md:2717:  if we go super exotic route, explain if this will still mean that each individual strix halo remains \"its own computer\" in a somewhat standalone way. this is a few paragraphs max\n/home/tom/trans.md:2889:  One pre-existing line is worth keeping, because it corroborates the throughput ceiling:\n/home/tom/trans.md:2893:  The kernel itself flags thunderbolt_net's receive offload as suspect. That is a driver-quality ceiling, not a link ceiling — and it is precisely what the RDMA stacks bypass.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3097:  Also worth noting from that same thread, a Framework Desktop owner: \"I tried TB networking on Framework Desktop, but it seems to be limited to 10 GB/s\" — the per-rail ceiling, independently hit by someone else on\n/home/tom/trans.md:3127:  cached bf16 dequant of the fp8 trunk) and what ROCmFPX proves works on this ISA at 88% of bandwidth ceiling. Whether vLLM's stock ROCm FP8 path does the smart thing on gfx1151 is unproven — AMD's reference\n/home/tom/trans.md:3161:  The thing worth internalising: in the last six months this hardware's ceiling moved 34 → 275 t/s prefill on unchanged silicon, and today your own link went 577 → 63 µs from a sysfs write. The gap between what\n/home/tom/trans.md:3276:  Dense at Q8: it fails your requirement, twice. 27B at Q8_0 is 28.7 GB against 32 GB of VRAM — about 3 GB left, which is roughly a 64K context ceiling at q4_0 KV, and ~17 t/s. Two R9700s cannot rescue this by\n/home/tom/trans.md:3304:  Nuance worth having: LLM decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, and a 300 W card at batch 1 may only pull 150–200 W anyway, so a 120 W ceiling may cost less than it sounds. Prefill is compute-bound and will take the\n/home/tom/trans.md:3438:  ceiling unpacking in VGPRs, and ds4-vllm already caches a BF16 dequant of an FP8 trunk. It's a software gap on a trodden path, not a wall.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3519:  ceiling unpacking in VGPRs, and ds4-vllm already caches a BF16 dequant of an FP8 trunk. It's a software gap on a trodden path, not a wall.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3530:  Register-time dequant. Weights stay FP8 in memory at 1 byte/param, unpacked to BF16 in VGPRs at the point of use. Footprint stays 172.78 GiB. This is what ROCmFPX does at 88% of bandwidth ceiling.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3625:  The card matters more than the switch. Your taps are PCIe 4.0 x4 (~63 Gbps ceiling), so you want a Gen4-capable card or the card's own link becomes the bottleneck. A ConnectX-3 is PCIe 3.0 x8 — in a Gen4 x4 slot\n/home/tom/trans.md:3631:  │ InfiniBand — SX6036    │ FDR 56 Gb/s, 36 ports │ ~€100–150          │ ~1.1 µs measured on Strix Halo │ Needs opensm; NCCL/RCCL speak IB natively. FDR 56 Gb ≈ your x4 ceiling — near-perfect match │\n/home/tom/trans.md:3639:  ceiling so nothing is wasted. The cost is running opensm and living with older silicon.\n/home/tom/trans.md:3659:  Decode ceiling: 18B active at FP8 ≈ 18 GB/token, quartered to 4.5 GB/node → ~21 ms → ~48 t/s before collectives. With an MTP layer and num_speculative_tokens: 5 on top.\n/home/tom/trans.md:4111:  Half the active parameters means roughly half the streamed bytes per token, which is roughly double the decode ceiling on a bandwidth-bound machine. That's an architecture advantage, not something waiting on\n/home/tom/trans.md:4139:  → 21.1 t/s ceiling; experts-sharded TP=2 = 8.91 GiB/token → 25.1 t/s ceiling. Halving the experts buys 16%. That is why Ran sees decode \"capped\" while prefill doubles.\n/home/tom/trans.md:4253:  Prefill will benefit. At a 2048-token chunk those same collectives carry 16 MB each — roughly 1.4 GB per chunk — and the per-rail ~10 Gb/s ceiling is genuinely binding. This is exactly the pain corruptedstack\n/home/tom/trans.md:4906:  I built the \"op assembly dominates wire time\" argument on those numbers. The structure survives — nhi_throttle.c:5-9 independently names 128 µs moderation against \"the measured ~65 µs mean one-way latency floor","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeYFKzDYodPX1Txqn3s6J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qs7pD6ZNPQikP3re5qfiyd","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 500,560p /home/tom/trans.md; echo \"=== 890-1000 ===\"; sed -n 895,1010p /home/tom/trans.md","description":"Read odinlink latency context"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3059,"cache_read_input_tokens":75389,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3059},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"7233f1cf-5157-42b8-ab2c-67dd47fbc365","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:17.774Z","request_id":"req_011CeYFKyPSSbyhZ96d1YYas"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qs7pD6ZNPQikP3re5qfiyd","type":"tool_result","content":"  14h ago\n  I'll go reading and experimenting! Thanks for the info.\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  u/LebiaseD avatar\n  LebiaseD\n  •\n  23h ago\n  What's your latency between devices?\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  vulpes\n  •\n  20h ago\n  11 μs according to this (One way), compared to 1.4 µs for CX7 on the spark.\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  u/PowerfulButterfly209 avatar\n  PowerfulButterfly209\n  •\n  23h ago\n  bosgame m5 is the cheapest option in EU\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  King_Kasma99\n=== 890-1000 ===\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  One-Cry297\n  •\n  22h ago\n  Nice work! What is the typical latency you get from Odinlink there?\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  Gobra_Slo\n  •\n  20h ago\n  The performance is just sad.\n\n  Good new is that a single Strix Halo with DSV4F in something like Q3_S has pretty similar speed, and I personally find DeepSeek to be quite good at this quant. In my opinion, there is no need to cluster two\n  machines, one is enough.\n\n  For clustering, DGX Spark is incomparably better, I get > 1500 on prefill and 60+ on generation, with cumulative generation scaling up to twice higher for few concurrent requests.\n\n  this user mentions https://github.com/Nathanw1014/llama.cpp/tree/strix-halo-vulkan (you can git clone and inspec ttaht)\n\n  https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/ark/products/series/225929/thunderbolt-5-controllers.html this is what is recommened to get more throughput: tell me if this would work for more 3 strix halo's? there was\n  an understanding that \"2 was the sweet spot and beyond that was useless\" somehow but perhaps htis has changed now:\n\n● Web Search(\"Intel Barlow Ridge Thunderbolt 5 controller JHL9580 add-in card AMD motherboard support PCIe\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 7s\n\n  Ran 2 shell commands (ctrl+o to expand)\n\n● Fetch(https://github.com/wkljohn/OdinLink-Five)\n  ⎿  Received 378.9KB (200 OK)\n\n● Web Search(\"Framework Desktop mainboard PCIe slot x4 Gen4 specification expansion\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 7s\n\n● Web Search(\"Strix Halo cluster 3 nodes 4 nodes scaling tensor parallel diminishing returns USB4 mesh topology 2026\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 9s\n\n● Web Search(\"ASMedia ASM4242 USB4 host controller PCIe add-in card AMD works without motherboard header\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 6s\n\n  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)\n\n● Cloned and read both repos plus OdinLink-Five. This changes one thing I told you earlier, and kills the TB5 idea for a reason that has nothing to do with Thunderbolt.\n\n  OdinLink is the actual news\n\n  I said USB4 gets you llama.cpp RPC pipeline-parallel but not tensor-parallel. That's now wrong. OdinLink-Five is an RDMA verbs provider (ibv_*, dma-buf zero-copy, RCCL plugin) that runs over your existing native\n  USB4 ports — the odl_tb5 naming is just the driver name; the README states testing was on USB4v1 cables between two Ryzen AI MAX+ 395s. No new hardware, no PCIe card. Real TP=2.\n\n  The reason it works is latency, not bandwidth, and that distinction drives everything below:\n\n  ┌───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┐\n  │                           │         OdinLink / USB4         │ Mellanox RoCE v2 │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ RTT                       │ 22 µs (vs 286 µs over TCP)      │ ~1.1 µs          │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ Bandwidth                 │ 8.38 Gb/s uni, 9.84 full-duplex │ ~50 Gb/s         │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ DS4 V4-Flash Q4_K prefill │ 233.04 t/s                      │ 275.58 t/s       │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ DS4 V4-Flash Q4_K decode  │ 19.17 t/s                       │ 20.43 t/s        │\n  └───────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┘\n\n  Baseline before any of this work: 34.11 t/s prefill, 9.96 decode.\n\n  So the entire delta from adding a real RDMA NIC is +18% prefill, +6.6% decode. Decode is latency-bound on small per-layer all-reduces, so both transports land in the same place. Prefill is bandwidth-bound on\n  2048-token chunks, which is where RoCE's 5× link speed shows up — and even then only as 18%.\n\n  Also worth knowing: the Antirez Q4_K GGUF is 164.6 GB against a 96 GiB ROCm aperture per node. For that model TP=2 isn't an optimization, it's the entry ticket. (The honest counterpoint is in the thread —\n  Gobra_Slo argues a single box at Q3_S gets comparable speed and you skip the whole exercise.)\n\n  The TB5 controller won't work — three independent blockers\n\n  The Intel ARK page you linked is a controller family page: JHL9580 (host) and JHL9480 (accessory) are chips OEMs solder down. Retrofitting means an add-in card, and:\n\n  1. Framework Desktop's slot is PCIe 4.0 x4, x4 physical, and closed-back. Only x4-length cards physically insert. This is already the constraint kyuz0's E810 had to satisfy.\n  2. TB5 AICs need a motherboard TBT header + BIOS support. ASUS ThunderboltEX 5 lists Intel 800 Series with a Thunderbolt (USB4) header as the requirement; it needs that header, a USB 2.0 header, and a 6-pin.\n  Framework Desktop has ATX headers, not a TBT header. Gigabyte's card is the same story.\n  3. The slot caps you anyway. Gen4 x4 ≈ 7.8 GB/s ≈ 62 Gbps, below TB5's 80. The \"120 Gbps\" figure is asymmetric display-out mode — irrelevant here.\n\n  wkljohn's comment (\"40gbps p2p can only do 20gbps, unless you have a tb5 controller you can do 80gbps\") describes what a host with native TB5 silicon can do. Strix Halo has USB4 v1 and you cannot bolt TB5 on.\n\n  An ASMedia ASM4242 USB4 AIC would work on AMD without a header — but it gives you more USB4 ports, not faster ones. Useful for topology, useless for throughput.\n\n  If you want past USB4 on this platform, the PCIe x4 slot with an RDMA NIC is the only path — which is exactly what both kyuz0 and wkljohn did. It's gotten cheap: the Strix Halo wiki documents ConnectX-3 CX354A at\n  €133 total for two nodes (2× cards €46, DAC €18, oculink adapters €49, PSU/cables €20) for 1.12 µs RTT. That's 20× better latency than OdinLink for €133.\n\n  Three boxes: \"2 is the sweet spot\" still holds\n\n  Four separate reasons, and none of them is software immaturity:\n\n  - Topology. 2 USB4 ports per box → 3 nodes form a full triangle mesh with no switch. That part genuinely works. 4 nodes can't full-mesh (needs 3 ports each), and USB4 host-to-host doesn't route, so you'd get a\n  ring with 2-hop paths.\n  - Software. The DS4 fork says it outright: \"This fork is for dual-Strix-Halo TP=2 over OdinLink or RoCE v2.\" No TP=3 or TP=4 path exists in it. I grepped — there's no world-size abstraction, it's two-rank\n  throughout.\n  - Arithmetic. TP shards attention heads and experts, and those counts are powers of two. TP=3 doesn't divide evenly for essentially any model. Your real options are 2 or 4, never 3.\n  - Scaling. Decode is already latency-bound at 20 t/s with 22 µs RTT. A TP=4 ring all-reduce adds hops at ~9 Gbps each. More ranks makes decode worse, not better.\n\n  What changed since kyuz0's video isn't that 3+ nodes became viable — it's how much better 2 nodes got (34 → 275 t/s prefill). A third box earns its keep as an independent workload (separate llama-swap server,\n  draft-model host, dev box), not as a third TP rank.\n\n  The Nathanw1014 branch — not what the name suggests","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"b32edbdf-26ee-4711-bb75-db94789a0b8e","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:19.256Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  14h ago\n  I'll go reading and experimenting! Thanks for the info.\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  u/LebiaseD avatar\n  LebiaseD\n  •\n  23h ago\n  What's your latency between devices?\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  vulpes\n  •\n  20h ago\n  11 μs according to this (One way), compared to 1.4 µs for CX7 on the spark.\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  u/PowerfulButterfly209 avatar\n  PowerfulButterfly209\n  •\n  23h ago\n  bosgame m5 is the cheapest option in EU\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  King_Kasma99\n=== 890-1000 ===\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  One-Cry297\n  •\n  22h ago\n  Nice work! What is the typical latency you get from Odinlink there?\n\n\n  UpvoteVote\n  Downvote\n\n  Reply\n\n  Award\n\n  Share\n\n  Gobra_Slo\n  •\n  20h ago\n  The performance is just sad.\n\n  Good new is that a single Strix Halo with DSV4F in something like Q3_S has pretty similar speed, and I personally find DeepSeek to be quite good at this quant. In my opinion, there is no need to cluster two\n  machines, one is enough.\n\n  For clustering, DGX Spark is incomparably better, I get > 1500 on prefill and 60+ on generation, with cumulative generation scaling up to twice higher for few concurrent requests.\n\n  this user mentions https://github.com/Nathanw1014/llama.cpp/tree/strix-halo-vulkan (you can git clone and inspec ttaht)\n\n  https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/ark/products/series/225929/thunderbolt-5-controllers.html this is what is recommened to get more throughput: tell me if this would work for more 3 strix halo's? there was\n  an understanding that \"2 was the sweet spot and beyond that was useless\" somehow but perhaps htis has changed now:\n\n● Web Search(\"Intel Barlow Ridge Thunderbolt 5 controller JHL9580 add-in card AMD motherboard support PCIe\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 7s\n\n  Ran 2 shell commands (ctrl+o to expand)\n\n● Fetch(https://github.com/wkljohn/OdinLink-Five)\n  ⎿  Received 378.9KB (200 OK)\n\n● Web Search(\"Framework Desktop mainboard PCIe slot x4 Gen4 specification expansion\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 7s\n\n● Web Search(\"Strix Halo cluster 3 nodes 4 nodes scaling tensor parallel diminishing returns USB4 mesh topology 2026\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 9s\n\n● Web Search(\"ASMedia ASM4242 USB4 host controller PCIe add-in card AMD works without motherboard header\")\n  ⎿  Did 1 search in 6s\n\n  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)\n\n● Cloned and read both repos plus OdinLink-Five. This changes one thing I told you earlier, and kills the TB5 idea for a reason that has nothing to do with Thunderbolt.\n\n  OdinLink is the actual news\n\n  I said USB4 gets you llama.cpp RPC pipeline-parallel but not tensor-parallel. That's now wrong. OdinLink-Five is an RDMA verbs provider (ibv_*, dma-buf zero-copy, RCCL plugin) that runs over your existing native\n  USB4 ports — the odl_tb5 naming is just the driver name; the README states testing was on USB4v1 cables between two Ryzen AI MAX+ 395s. No new hardware, no PCIe card. Real TP=2.\n\n  The reason it works is latency, not bandwidth, and that distinction drives everything below:\n\n  ┌───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┐\n  │                           │         OdinLink / USB4         │ Mellanox RoCE v2 │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ RTT                       │ 22 µs (vs 286 µs over TCP)      │ ~1.1 µs          │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ Bandwidth                 │ 8.38 Gb/s uni, 9.84 full-duplex │ ~50 Gb/s         │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ DS4 V4-Flash Q4_K prefill │ 233.04 t/s                      │ 275.58 t/s       │\n  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n  │ DS4 V4-Flash Q4_K decode  │ 19.17 t/s                       │ 20.43 t/s        │\n  └───────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┘\n\n  Baseline before any of this work: 34.11 t/s prefill, 9.96 decode.\n\n  So the entire delta from adding a real RDMA NIC is +18% prefill, +6.6% decode. Decode is latency-bound on small per-layer all-reduces, so both transports land in the same place. Prefill is bandwidth-bound on\n  2048-token chunks, which is where RoCE's 5× link speed shows up — and even then only as 18%.\n\n  Also worth knowing: the Antirez Q4_K GGUF is 164.6 GB against a 96 GiB ROCm aperture per node. For that model TP=2 isn't an optimization, it's the entry ticket. (The honest counterpoint is in the thread —\n  Gobra_Slo argues a single box at Q3_S gets comparable speed and you skip the whole exercise.)\n\n  The TB5 controller won't work — three independent blockers\n\n  The Intel ARK page you linked is a controller family page: JHL9580 (host) and JHL9480 (accessory) are chips OEMs solder down. Retrofitting means an add-in card, and:\n\n  1. Framework Desktop's slot is PCIe 4.0 x4, x4 physical, and closed-back. Only x4-length cards physically insert. This is already the constraint kyuz0's E810 had to satisfy.\n  2. TB5 AICs need a motherboard TBT header + BIOS support. ASUS ThunderboltEX 5 lists Intel 800 Series with a Thunderbolt (USB4) header as the requirement; it needs that header, a USB 2.0 header, and a 6-pin.\n  Framework Desktop has ATX headers, not a TBT header. Gigabyte's card is the same story.\n  3. The slot caps you anyway. Gen4 x4 ≈ 7.8 GB/s ≈ 62 Gbps, below TB5's 80. The \"120 Gbps\" figure is asymmetric display-out mode — irrelevant here.\n\n  wkljohn's comment (\"40gbps p2p can only do 20gbps, unless you have a tb5 controller you can do 80gbps\") describes what a host with native TB5 silicon can do. Strix Halo has USB4 v1 and you cannot bolt TB5 on.\n\n  An ASMedia ASM4242 USB4 AIC would work on AMD without a header — but it gives you more USB4 ports, not faster ones. Useful for topology, useless for throughput.\n\n  If you want past USB4 on this platform, the PCIe x4 slot with an RDMA NIC is the only path — which is exactly what both kyuz0 and wkljohn did. It's gotten cheap: the Strix Halo wiki documents ConnectX-3 CX354A at\n  €133 total for two nodes (2× cards €46, DAC €18, oculink adapters €49, PSU/cables €20) for 1.12 µs RTT. That's 20× better latency than OdinLink for €133.\n\n  Three boxes: \"2 is the sweet spot\" still holds\n\n  Four separate reasons, and none of them is software immaturity:\n\n  - Topology. 2 USB4 ports per box → 3 nodes form a full triangle mesh with no switch. That part genuinely works. 4 nodes can't full-mesh (needs 3 ports each), and USB4 host-to-host doesn't route, so you'd get a\n  ring with 2-hop paths.\n  - Software. The DS4 fork says it outright: \"This fork is for dual-Strix-Halo TP=2 over OdinLink or RoCE v2.\" No TP=3 or TP=4 path exists in it. I grepped — there's no world-size abstraction, it's two-rank\n  throughout.\n  - Arithmetic. TP shards attention heads and experts, and those counts are powers of two. TP=3 doesn't divide evenly for essentially any model. Your real options are 2 or 4, never 3.\n  - Scaling. Decode is already latency-bound at 20 t/s with 22 µs RTT. A TP=4 ring all-reduce adds hops at ~9 Gbps each. More ranks makes decode worse, not better.\n\n  What changed since kyuz0's video isn't that 3+ nodes became viable — it's how much better 2 nodes got (34 → 275 t/s prefill). A third box earns its keep as an independent workload (separate llama-swap server,\n  draft-model host, dev box), not as a third TP rank.\n\n  The Nathanw1014 branch — not what the name suggests","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
