{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeYFLFxqiLJDanmJ3g8ms","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3540,"cache_read_input_tokens":78448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3540},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"25b8899d-5d36-4b80-b2f5-e5783f14e6a4","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:24.624Z","request_id":"req_011CeYFLEubBiYXtx3HsBoHw"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeYFLFxqiLJDanmJ3g8ms","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017E86y1MPpkMkRf54tTDg3N","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 320,420p /home/tom/additional-research.md","description":"Read additional-research odinlink section"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3540,"cache_read_input_tokens":78448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3540},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"8afe4f97-d6f9-41cf-9899-b1830ac22410","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:24.988Z","request_id":"req_011CeYFLEubBiYXtx3HsBoHw"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017E86y1MPpkMkRf54tTDg3N","type":"tool_result","content":"40 Gb/s rail (~5 GB/s), that is **~200× headroom**; at 500 t/s concurrent, still ~20×. The\nbandwidth conclusion holds by a wider margin than claimed.\n\n**It models wire latency, not op latency.** An RDMA all-reduce is not one wire crossing.\n`ds4-vllm`'s `tbv_ar2` claims a complete op in ~105 µs against ~3.3 µs of wire time — the\nrest is staging copies, doorbell kernel launches, progress-thread wakeups, GPU polling.\nThat overhead does not shrink when the wire does. (And the ~105 µs is itself **unmeasured**\nby the patch's own admission at `vllm-upstream.patch:1557–1559`, while the repo's docstrings\nstill state it as fact.)\n\nThe direct A/B, same rig and settings: **TCP 8.29 decode vs OdinLink RDMA 8.57 — +3.4%**.\nThe repo's own conclusion: *\"the wire was never the limiter.\"*\n\n**\"As if they were a single unified 256 GB GPU\" is false** even at 1.15 µs. TP=2 shards the\nexperts but not the always-read trunk. Byte accounting: no TP = 10.62 GiB/token → 21.1 t/s\nceiling; experts-sharded TP=2 = 8.91 GiB/token → 25.1 t/s. Halving the experts buys 16% —\nwhich is why prefill roughly doubles while decode stays capped.\n\n### Three links, three jobs\n\n**Decode does 96 sequential *dependent* collectives** — each blocks on the previous, so the\ntail accumulates rather than the average. At that shape the 5GbE (75 µs avg, 6 µs mdev) is\ncurrently better-behaved than Thunderbolt (90 µs avg, 28 µs mdev), despite 8× less\nbandwidth. Thunderbolt's 32 µs floor is the real hardware; the 28 µs jitter is the NHI's\nhardcoded ~128 µs MSI-X moderation window.\n\nPrefill inverts it: at a 2048-token chunk those same collectives carry ~16 MB each, ~1.4 GB\nper chunk. Pure bandwidth, and Thunderbolt wins 8×.\n\nAssignments:\n\n- **5GbE → control plane.** Lowest jitter, and moving IP off the TB rails frees NHI ring\n  capacity. Ring exhaustion is precisely why `ds4-vllm` invented `cables: 2`; an idle 5GbE\n  link solves it for free. Currently the control plane rides `thunderbolt0` instead, and the\n  5GbE has moved ~290 KB in its lifetime.\n- **Two TB rails → RDMA only.** Both trained at 40 Gb/s on separate domains with independent\n  NHIs. This is the hellas-ai reference config — their ~48 Gb/s/direction and\n  \"TP=2 beats single-node at batch=1\" headlines are 4-HCA aggregate, i.e. two cables.\n- **Neither rail needs replacing** at TP=2. See below.\n\n### Do we still need OCuLink / InfiniBand? At TP=2, no.\n\nModeled for Flash-Next (per-node ~3 GB streamed/token ÷ 215 GB/s = 14 ms compute):\n\n| Fabric | Collective term | Total | Decode |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Current tuned TCP, 69 µs | 6.6 ms | 20.6 ms | ~48 t/s |\n| Soft-RDMA, 22 µs | 2.1 ms | 16.1 ms | ~62 t/s |\n| InfiniBand, 1.1 µs | 0.1 ms | 14.1 ms | ~71 t/s |\n\nThe whole fabric spectrum is worth ~48%, and the free two-thirds of it — installing\n`thunderbolt-ibverbs` on cables already owned — captures 48 → 62. A €133 ConnectX-3 +\nOCuLink build buys the remaining **~13%**.\n\nCaveat cutting against the table: it assumes op cost tracks wire cost, and the only direct\nA/B measurement (+3.4%) says it does not. The real prize is the ~105 µs of op assembly\nabove the wire — which is software, and whose own v3 prescription starts with the C-state\nhold already applied, then pinning the progress thread and dropping its `usleep(200)`\nbackoff.\n\n**Where the fabric does become mandatory: TP=4**, where a ring all-reduce goes from 2 steps\nto 6. Four boxes on USB4 measures slower at decode than two.\n\n### Second-cable caveats\n\nBoth cables are currently connected and both trained cleanly — the hopid collision\ndocumented in `tbv`'s README did **not** occur at the plain `thunderbolt_net` layer, because\nthe links land on separate domains. **This is untested for the RDMA control handler.**\nOdinLink is documented-broken with two cables (\"both peer services sit at route=2… use 1\nwith two cables\") and is TB5/MS-S1-targeted; `thunderbolt-ibverbs` is the multi-rail stack\nand was tested on this exact silicon.\n\nThe unbuilt idea worth noting: striping odd/even layers across the two rails so consecutive\nall-reduces pipeline rather than queue. That attacks latency directly. Nobody has built it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Corrections to the prior record\n\nThings stated earlier in this line of work that later evidence changed:\n\n1. **\"~12% decode cost for SSD mode.\"** Unsupported, and so is the ~21% reading that\n   replaced it. 396 vs 502 t/s compares a *server* run against a *llama-bench* run —\n   EngramHalo's README says so explicitly. SSD-mode cost is not cleanly measured anywhere.\n   The IOPS arithmetic in §4 suggests it should be small.\n2. **\"The engram is one hash table.\"** It is **16**.\n3. **\"It fires at layer 2.\"** `ple_layer_ids` is **1-based** — off by one.\n4. **\"The 1 TB SKU's larger FTL DRAM is the argument.\"** Neutral. 512 MB fully caches a\n   500 GB mapping table. Presence of DRAM is decisive; size is not.\n5. **\"62.5 GiB doesn't appear in the sources.\"** It does, and it reconciles: 172.78 GiB\n   total − ~47.7 GiB engram = 125.08 GiB non-engram, halved at TP=2 = **62.5 GiB per rank**.\n   That is the vLLM whole-model-minus-engram figure. The **~1 GiB** is the llama.cpp\n   engram-only resident measurement. Different quantities, both correct.\n6. **\"RAM mode is a viable speed/memory trade.\"** It **deadlocks** above ~128K context.\n7. **\"OdinLink is a candidate.\"** Documented-broken with two cables, which is the current\n   configuration.\n8. **\"Fork `antirez/ds4`.\"** llama.cpp cannot do cross-host tensor parallelism (RPC is\n   pipeline/layer-split only), and `antirez/ds4` is a DeepSeek-V4-specific engine. The fork\n   target is `AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm`, ~70% of whose tree (transport, `tbv`, build machinery) is\n   model-agnostic.\n9. **\"The FP8 dequant dilemma.\"** Stock vLLM does neither silently: `supports_fp8()` returns","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ef1d37d-6f19-4b92-8ee7-3ab4af23febd","uuid":"4c0f5c14-6d38-43e3-abf6-5e15c485f128","timestamp":"2026-08-30T03:47:26.961Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"40 Gb/s rail (~5 GB/s), that is **~200× headroom**; at 500 t/s concurrent, still ~20×. The\nbandwidth conclusion holds by a wider margin than claimed.\n\n**It models wire latency, not op latency.** An RDMA all-reduce is not one wire crossing.\n`ds4-vllm`'s `tbv_ar2` claims a complete op in ~105 µs against ~3.3 µs of wire time — the\nrest is staging copies, doorbell kernel launches, progress-thread wakeups, GPU polling.\nThat overhead does not shrink when the wire does. (And the ~105 µs is itself **unmeasured**\nby the patch's own admission at `vllm-upstream.patch:1557–1559`, while the repo's docstrings\nstill state it as fact.)\n\nThe direct A/B, same rig and settings: **TCP 8.29 decode vs OdinLink RDMA 8.57 — +3.4%**.\nThe repo's own conclusion: *\"the wire was never the limiter.\"*\n\n**\"As if they were a single unified 256 GB GPU\" is false** even at 1.15 µs. TP=2 shards the\nexperts but not the always-read trunk. Byte accounting: no TP = 10.62 GiB/token → 21.1 t/s\nceiling; experts-sharded TP=2 = 8.91 GiB/token → 25.1 t/s. Halving the experts buys 16% —\nwhich is why prefill roughly doubles while decode stays capped.\n\n### Three links, three jobs\n\n**Decode does 96 sequential *dependent* collectives** — each blocks on the previous, so the\ntail accumulates rather than the average. At that shape the 5GbE (75 µs avg, 6 µs mdev) is\ncurrently better-behaved than Thunderbolt (90 µs avg, 28 µs mdev), despite 8× less\nbandwidth. Thunderbolt's 32 µs floor is the real hardware; the 28 µs jitter is the NHI's\nhardcoded ~128 µs MSI-X moderation window.\n\nPrefill inverts it: at a 2048-token chunk those same collectives carry ~16 MB each, ~1.4 GB\nper chunk. Pure bandwidth, and Thunderbolt wins 8×.\n\nAssignments:\n\n- **5GbE → control plane.** Lowest jitter, and moving IP off the TB rails frees NHI ring\n  capacity. Ring exhaustion is precisely why `ds4-vllm` invented `cables: 2`; an idle 5GbE\n  link solves it for free. Currently the control plane rides `thunderbolt0` instead, and the\n  5GbE has moved ~290 KB in its lifetime.\n- **Two TB rails → RDMA only.** Both trained at 40 Gb/s on separate domains with independent\n  NHIs. This is the hellas-ai reference config — their ~48 Gb/s/direction and\n  \"TP=2 beats single-node at batch=1\" headlines are 4-HCA aggregate, i.e. two cables.\n- **Neither rail needs replacing** at TP=2. See below.\n\n### Do we still need OCuLink / InfiniBand? At TP=2, no.\n\nModeled for Flash-Next (per-node ~3 GB streamed/token ÷ 215 GB/s = 14 ms compute):\n\n| Fabric | Collective term | Total | Decode |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Current tuned TCP, 69 µs | 6.6 ms | 20.6 ms | ~48 t/s |\n| Soft-RDMA, 22 µs | 2.1 ms | 16.1 ms | ~62 t/s |\n| InfiniBand, 1.1 µs | 0.1 ms | 14.1 ms | ~71 t/s |\n\nThe whole fabric spectrum is worth ~48%, and the free two-thirds of it — installing\n`thunderbolt-ibverbs` on cables already owned — captures 48 → 62. A €133 ConnectX-3 +\nOCuLink build buys the remaining **~13%**.\n\nCaveat cutting against the table: it assumes op cost tracks wire cost, and the only direct\nA/B measurement (+3.4%) says it does not. The real prize is the ~105 µs of op assembly\nabove the wire — which is software, and whose own v3 prescription starts with the C-state\nhold already applied, then pinning the progress thread and dropping its `usleep(200)`\nbackoff.\n\n**Where the fabric does become mandatory: TP=4**, where a ring all-reduce goes from 2 steps\nto 6. Four boxes on USB4 measures slower at decode than two.\n\n### Second-cable caveats\n\nBoth cables are currently connected and both trained cleanly — the hopid collision\ndocumented in `tbv`'s README did **not** occur at the plain `thunderbolt_net` layer, because\nthe links land on separate domains. **This is untested for the RDMA control handler.**\nOdinLink is documented-broken with two cables (\"both peer services sit at route=2… use 1\nwith two cables\") and is TB5/MS-S1-targeted; `thunderbolt-ibverbs` is the multi-rail stack\nand was tested on this exact silicon.\n\nThe unbuilt idea worth noting: striping odd/even layers across the two rails so consecutive\nall-reduces pipeline rather than queue. That attacks latency directly. Nobody has built it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Corrections to the prior record\n\nThings stated earlier in this line of work that later evidence changed:\n\n1. **\"~12% decode cost for SSD mode.\"** Unsupported, and so is the ~21% reading that\n   replaced it. 396 vs 502 t/s compares a *server* run against a *llama-bench* run —\n   EngramHalo's README says so explicitly. SSD-mode cost is not cleanly measured anywhere.\n   The IOPS arithmetic in §4 suggests it should be small.\n2. **\"The engram is one hash table.\"** It is **16**.\n3. **\"It fires at layer 2.\"** `ple_layer_ids` is **1-based** — off by one.\n4. **\"The 1 TB SKU's larger FTL DRAM is the argument.\"** Neutral. 512 MB fully caches a\n   500 GB mapping table. Presence of DRAM is decisive; size is not.\n5. **\"62.5 GiB doesn't appear in the sources.\"** It does, and it reconciles: 172.78 GiB\n   total − ~47.7 GiB engram = 125.08 GiB non-engram, halved at TP=2 = **62.5 GiB per rank**.\n   That is the vLLM whole-model-minus-engram figure. The **~1 GiB** is the llama.cpp\n   engram-only resident measurement. Different quantities, both correct.\n6. **\"RAM mode is a viable speed/memory trade.\"** It **deadlocks** above ~128K context.\n7. **\"OdinLink is a candidate.\"** Documented-broken with two cables, which is the current\n   configuration.\n8. **\"Fork `antirez/ds4`.\"** llama.cpp cannot do cross-host tensor parallelism (RPC is\n   pipeline/layer-split only), and `antirez/ds4` is a DeepSeek-V4-specific engine. The fork\n   target is `AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm`, ~70% of whose tree (transport, `tbv`, build machinery) is\n   model-agnostic.\n9. **\"The FP8 dequant dilemma.\"** Stock vLLM does neither silently: `supports_fp8()` returns","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
